==German==

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|goh|de}} {{term|sizzen|lang=goh}}, originally *{{term||sitten}}, from {{etyl|gem-pro|de}} {{m|gem-pro|*sitjaną}}, from {{etyl|ine-pro|de}} {{m|ine-pro|*sed-}}.  Cognate with Low German {{term|sitten|lang=nds}}, Dutch {{term|zitten|lang=nl}}, English {{term|sit|lang=en}}, Danish {{term|sidde|lang=da}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/ˈzɪtsn̩/|/ˈzɪtsən/|lang=de}}
* {{audio|De-sitzen.ogg|audio|lang=de}}

===Verb===
{{de-verb-strong|class=5|sitzt|saß|gesessen|past subjunctive=säße}}

# {{context|intransitive|lang=de}} to [[sit]]; to [[perch]]
# {{context|intransitive|lang=de}} to [[stay]] (in one place); to [[remain]]; to [[be]] (in a particular place or state)
# {{context|intransitive|of clothing|lang=de}} to [[fit]]
# {{context|intransitive|colloquial|lang=de}} to [[do time]]; to [[spend]] time in jail
# {{context|intransitive|colloquial|of a strike, a comment, etc.|lang=de}} to [[hit home]]; to have a significant effect

====Conjugation====
{{de-conj-strong|sitz|saß|gesessen|h|||säß|a|a|||a}}

====Usage notes====
The standard auxiliary verb used with the past participle {{term|gesessen}} is {{term|haben}}.  In Upper German dialects (High German dialects spoken primarily in Austria, Switzerland, southern Germany, and Northern Italy), the auxiliary verb {{term|sein}} is used instead.

